{:edb/db-before-t 1034 :edb/db-after-t 1035 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534347 50 #inst "2026-10-11T19:27:32.092Z" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131036 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419881 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131036 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419881 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131062 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419870 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131062 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419870 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131063 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419908 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186131063 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419908 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133267 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133268 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133270 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186133271 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419865 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419888 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419891 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133266 1075 #edb/ref 17592186419898 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133266 1078 #edb/ref 17592186419898 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133516 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/17/1714eb4797f7b318f5e957db7ab0f322fffb2eb2c9bf03f68e3acc08ec399849.svg)\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133516 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${e^{-x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 0$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133516 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for\n\n$$\nx \\geq 0\n$$\n\nthe cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-t}dt \\\\\n&= \\left.- e^{-t}\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&=-e^{-x} + 1 \\\\\n&= 1 - e^{-x}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ 1 - e^{-x},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133516 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x \\geq 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/4f/4f0d336b322f68550c50d65380e26ffb33bdd55beab0c7d429e7b4055f973b57.svg)\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/9e/9e1b63a37177781672b70c3a6c01b1b752164b976b6e9982e07d510d468353aa.svg)" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133517 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x) = e^{-|2x|}, \\quad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133517 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = e^{-\\mid 2x \\mid}, \\quad x \\in (-\\infty, \\infty)$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133517 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/40/40ebced9e322ef969e40733356aacdb3971e14276bc6ad54691aeea21b14c488.svg)\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x \\leq 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2t}}{2}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\frac{{e}^{2x}}{2}|}_{x=0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $x > 0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =-{\\frac{{e}^{-2t}}{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}{e}^{2t}\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}{e}^{-2t}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{1}{2}+(-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =1-\\frac{{e}^{-2x}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/92/9293ff0dab0e6c12fd143ec5f8a9d72edfce74fcddae2397f19d0e82a8b767cc.svg)" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133517 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that involves an absolute value, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{\\int_{- ∞}^{x}e^{2t}dt,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt + \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$\n\nLet's compute the integrals.\n\n- If $x \\leq 0$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{x}e^{2t}dt|= \\left.\\frac{e^{2t}}{2}\\right|_{- ∞}^{x}; = \\frac{e^{2x}}{2}$,\nTherefore,\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt|= \\left.\\frac{e^{2x}}{2}\\right|_{x = 0}; = \\frac{1}{2}$.\n- If $x > 0$, we have\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt|= \\left.- \\frac{e^{-2t}}{2}\\right|_{0}^{x}; =-\\frac{e^{-2x}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}$.\nAs a result, we get\n$\\displaystyle \\int_{- ∞}^{0}e^{2t}dt + \\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{x}e^{-2t}dt|= \\frac{1}{2} + (-\\frac{e^{-2x}}{2} + \\frac{1}{2}); = 1 - \\frac{e^{-2x}}{2}$.\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{\\frac{e^{2x}}{2},\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ 1 - \\frac{e^{-2x}}{2},\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133518 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n![](images/9d/9d755ff833ca8a9134d8f2548b6e7e26221a4814cda8a28093a345a2d321920e.svg)\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133518 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n${\\frac{1}{72}x,\\begin{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12, \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133518 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x \\leq 12\n$$\n\nthe cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12; 1, \\mid x > 12\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt &= \\int_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{72}tdt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{1}{144}t^{2}\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{144}x^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ \\frac{x^{2}}{144},\\end{vmatrix}0 \\le x \\le 12; 1, \\mid x > 12\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133518 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $0 \\leq x \\leq 12,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/fd/fd6306e35f5c55b4de635fe7d8967e7783f11c96cee2ea8adfdd17348f97a095.svg)\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{1}{72}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{144}{t}^{2}|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{144}{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n![](images/b1/b168cb9b6e4619e7b25cddea9ee671e665d057923b9562ff50d99ce874ea8b90.svg)" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133519 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n![](images/97/9792a5033ccd5f6f8198e3465899c752ddd4ae0986b324c13575c1a92e1ff3c7.svg)\n\nfind $P(2\\leq X \\leq 6).$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133519 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the cumulative distribution function\n${0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 5 \\\\ \\frac{x^{2} - 25}{75},\\end{vmatrix}5 \\le x \\le 10; 1, \\mid x > 10$,\n\nfind $P(2\\leq X \\leq 6)$." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133519 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(2 \\le X \\le 6) &= P(2 < X \\le 6) \\\\\n&= P(X \\le 6) - P(X \\le 2) \\\\\n&= F(6) - F(2) \\\\\n&= \\frac{6^{2} - 25}{75} - 0 \\\\\n&= \\frac{11}{75}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186133519 1074 "Remember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval. So we can perform this computation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(2\\le X\\le 6) & =P(2<X\\le 6) \\\\  & =P(X\\le 6)-P(X\\le 2) \\\\  & =F(6)-F(2) \\\\  & =\\frac{{6}^{2}-25}{75}-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{75}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419947 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419952 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133726 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419953 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419950 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419951 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419956 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133727 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419958 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419943 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419949 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419954 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133728 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419955 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419944 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419945 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419946 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419948 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186133729 1038 #edb/ref 17592186419957 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134025 1050 1200.0 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134150 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134150 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{21}x,\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2 \\\\ h(x),\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134150 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = {\\frac{2}{21}x,\\begin{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}otherwise$\nits cumulative distribution function is\n$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 2 \\\\ h(x),\\end{vmatrix}2 \\le x \\le 5; 1, \\mid x > 5$\nwhere $h(x) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186134150 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $2\\le x\\le 5,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<2 \\\\ {\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & 2\\le x\\le 5 \\\\ 1, & x>5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{2}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{2}^{x}\\frac{2}{21}t\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={\\frac{1}{21}{t}^{2}|}_{2}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{21}{x}^{2}-\\frac{4}{21} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<2, \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}-4}{21},\\, & 2\\le x\\le 5, \\\\ 1,\\, & x>5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134158 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134158 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}6{e}^{-6x},\\, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<0 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $h(x)=${{field-1}}." 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134158 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = {6e^{-6x},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 0 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 0$\nits cumulative distribution function is\n$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0 \\\\ h(x),\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0$\nwhere $h(x) =$ {{field-1}}." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186134158 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 0,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0 \\\\ {\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{0}^{x}6{e}^{-6t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & =-{e}^{-6t}{|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-6x}+1 \\\\  & =1-{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<0, \\\\ 1-{e}^{-6x}, & x\\ge 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134159 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134159 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{{e}^{x}}{(1+{e}^{x})^{2}},\\,x\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),\n$$\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x).$\n\n$F(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134159 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = (e^{x})/((1 + e^{x})^{2}), x ∈ (- ∞, ∞)$,\n\nfind the cumulative distribution function $F(x)$.\n\n$F(x) =$\n{{field-1}}" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186134159 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a continuous random variable $X$ is the function $F(x)$ defined by\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}\\frac{{e}^{t}}{(1+{e}^{t})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{t}}|}_{-\\infty }^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{x}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{x}}{1+{e}^{x}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134538 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134538 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}},\\, & x\\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\, & x<1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nits cumulative distribution function is \n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0,\\, & x<1 \\\\ h(x),\\, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFind the function $h(x).$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186134538 1071 "Given that the random variable $X$ has the probability density function\n$f(x) = {\\frac{1}{x^{2}},\\begin{vmatrix}x \\ge 1 \\\\ 0,\\end{vmatrix}x < 1$\nits cumulative distribution function is\n$F(x) = {0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 1 \\\\ h(x),\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 1$.\nFind the function $h(x)$." 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186134538 1074 "Since our random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive only for $x\\ge 1,$ the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\nF(x)=P(X\\le x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1 \\\\ {\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t, & x\\ge 1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the given probability density function, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t & ={\\int }_{1}^{x}\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\text{ } & ={-\\frac{1}{t}|}_{1}^{x} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{x}+1 \\\\  & =1-\\frac{1}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the cumulative distribution function is given by\n\n$$\nF(x)={\\begin{aligned}0, & x<1, \\\\ 1-\\frac{1}{x}, & x\\ge 1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186135186 1064 "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable.\n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X$, the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x)\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x)$, then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration.\n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n$$\n{(2)/(π(1 + x^{2})), \\mid x ∈ [0, ∞); 0, \\mid otherwise\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\nf(x)= 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x < 0$, we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0$.\n\nTo compute the CDF for\n\n$$\nx\\geq 0\n$$\n\nwe integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(x) &= \\int_{- ∞}^{x}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= \\int_{- ∞}^{0}f(t)dt + \\int_{0}^{x}f(t)dt \\\\\n&= 0 + \\int_{0}^{x}(2)/(π(1 + t^{2}))dt \\\\\n&= \\left.\\frac{2}{π}\\arctan t\\right|_{0}^{x} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x - \\frac{2}{π}(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < 0, \\\\ \\frac{2}{π}\\arctan x,\\end{vmatrix}x \\ge 0\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186135186 1064 "The cumulative distribution function (or CDF) of a continuous random variable is defined the same way as for a discrete random variable. \n\nGiven a continuous random variable $X,$ the CDF is the function $F(x)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x).\n$$\n\nIf our random variable has the probability density function (or PDF) $f(x),$ then we have\n\n$$\nF(x) = P(X \\leq x) = \\int_{-\\infty}^x f(t) \\, \\textrm dt.\n$$\n\nNote that we use $f(t)dt$ in the integrand because $x$ serves as the upper limit of integration. \n\nFor example, consider the random variable $X$ with the following PDF:\n\n![](images/e7/e79c230f2b548c9280425f555f6d99a8f997e989289a05fb5f4584b656c4dcc8.svg)\n\nSince $f(x)= 0$ for all $x < 0,$  we have\n\n$$\nF(x)  = \\int_{-\\infty}^x 0 \\, \\textrm dt = 0\n$$\n\nif $x<0.$\n\nTo compute the CDF for $x\\geq 0,$  we integrate $f(x)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(x) & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t+{\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =0+{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{2}{\\pi (1+{t}^{2})}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡t|}_{0}^{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x-\\frac{2}{\\pi }(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\pi }\\operatorname{arctan}⁡x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing our CDF as a function defined over the entire real line, we get\n\n![](images/66/66b7bf54271075ae72fcf07fa4dc8573a94e97c2b2cc77680c0ec94b6053f360.svg)" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186135187 1064 "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval\n\n$$\na \\leq x \\leq b\n$$\n\nonly, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a$, and\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b$.\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n$$\n{0,\\begin{vmatrix}x < a \\\\ \\int_{a}^{x}f(t)dt,\\end{vmatrix}a \\le x \\le b; 1, \\mid x > b\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186135187 1064 "When a random variable $X$ has a probability distribution function $f(x)$ that is strictly positive on the interval $a \\leq x \\leq b$ only, we have\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 0$ for $x < a,$ and\n\n- $P(X \\leq x) = 1$ for $x > b.$\n\nIn these cases, the cumulative distribution function takes the following form:\n\n![](images/7a/7a64bac7c252a3f52fc92f6364edb5c66f31e47da28e78eeb0d069d894135700.svg)" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186135188 1064 "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq x \\leq 1)\n$$\n\nas follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP(0 \\le x \\le 1) &= F(1) - F(0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-1}} - \\frac{1}{1 + e^{0}} \\\\\n&= \\frac{1}{1 + e^{-1}} - \\frac{1}{2} \\\\\n&\\approx 0.231\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X$, we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 false] [17592186135188 1064 "As with discrete random variables, we can use the CDF of a continuous random variable to quickly compute a probability over an interval:\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = F(b) - F(a)\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a random variable $X$ has the following CDF:\n\n$$\nF(x) = \\dfrac{1}{1+e^{-x}}, \\qquad -\\infty < x < \\infty.\n$$\n\nWe can use the above CDF to quickly compute $P(0 \\leq x \\leq 1),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0\\le x\\le 1) & =F(1)-F(0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1+{e}^{-1}}-\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & ≈0.231\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRemember that for continuous random variables, it doesn't matter whether or not we include the endpoints of the interval, so we have\n\n$$\nP(0 \\leq X \\leq 1) = P(0 < X \\leq 1) = P(0 \\leq X < 1) = P(0 < X < 1) = 0.231.\n$$\n\nIn general, for a continuous random variable $X,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(a \\leq X \\leq b) = P(a < X \\leq b) = P(a \\leq X < b) = P(a < X < b) = F(b) - F(a).\n$$" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419856 1080 "\\frac{7}{16}" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419856 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419856 1083 #uuid "04fc2411-0461-5350-a6d4-77130a96a596" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419857 1080 "F(x)=1-\\frac{1}{1-{e}^{x}}" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419857 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419857 1083 #uuid "095360e1-dd96-5383-931f-a201eff47a39" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419858 1080 "F(x)=\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{2x})^{4}}" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419858 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419858 1083 #uuid "0b1e4645-ce48-5f06-a7d7-34323e0d0b93" 13194139534347 17592186045417 true] [17592186419859 1080 "F(x)=1-\\frac{1}{(1+{e}^{2x})^{2}}" 1